{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And you came to this place.</b> Moses introduced these two verses in the midst of his words because he wished to strengthen their hearts regarding the inheritance of the land. And just as they inherited the land of Sihon and Og, who were among the seven nations, and He struck them and took hold of their land already, and it became an inheritance for two and a half tribes, so too in the future they would inherit the rest across the Jordan — the nine and a half tribes." "<b>And know</b> that the rebukes in this parashah were said in the language of Moses — not that Moses spoke of his own accord, for we have a received tradition that the entire Torah, from Genesis through לעיני כל ישראל, Moses wrote all of it from the mouth of the Almighty. And what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded — that the curses in the Priests' Torah (Torat Kohanim) the Holy One, blessed be He, uttered, while those in Mishneh Torah Moses uttered of his own accord — the intent is that in these he spoke 'of his own accord,' meaning that his own mind concurred with the mind of the Holy One, blessed be He. And the reason the curses in the Priests' Torah were expressed in the language of the Holy One, blessed be He, while these in Mishneh Torah are in the language of Moses, is that those curses in the Priests' Torah were fulfilled in the First Temple, where there were idol worshippers — as He mentioned among them (Leviticus 26:30): ״והשמדתי את במותיכם והכרתי את חמניכם״ — for the Shekhinah dwelt in the First Temple; but these were all fulfilled in the Second Temple, and in the Second Temple the Shekhinah had withdrawn. Therefore the expression here comes in the language of Moses and not in the language of the Shekhinah. And I have already mentioned this in the section of אם בחקותי." "<b>And you must</b> know that because the Shekhinah dwelt in the First Temple, therefore David, peace be upon him, said concerning the First Temple (Psalms 24:7): ״שאו שערים ראשיכם והנשאו פתחי עולם ויבא מלך הכבוד״ — that is, the King of the Glory; but in the Second Temple the Shekhinah did not dwell so constantly but rather hovered, as the prophet Haggai prophesied concerning the Second Temple (Haggai 1:1): ״וארצה בו״ — ״ונכבד אמר ה׳״, that is, the glory of the Shekhinah, which is the letter heh, hovered there. And he said as well (Haggai 2:): ״ומלאתי את הבית הזה כבוד״. And in the time to come all will return to its former state as it was in the First Temple, and with surpassing excellence and attainment beyond what it had been, and the תפארת and the כבוד shall return to Jerusalem. And this is what Isaiah, peace be upon him, said concerning the Third Temple (Isaiah 60:1–2): ״קומי אורי כי בא אורך וכבוד ה׳ עליך זרח, כי הנה החשך יכסה ארץ וערפל לאומים ועליך יזרח ה׳ וכבודו עליך יראה״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {2 "The Hebrew text appears to read וארצה בו ואכדה אמר ה׳; the second word may be a corruption of ונכבד. The Haggai citation is given as א׳:א׳ but the content does not correspond to Haggai 1:1; the phrase וארצה בו appears in Haggai 1:8. Translated hyper-literally as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:57.704011230Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}